\label{p2}
Let the set $D$, as a subset of $X$, be dense in itself.
Furthermore, let there be a sequence of neural networks $\{NN^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}:\, Y\to D$ solving the inverse problem on the set $D$ with individual accuracy $\varepsilon_n > 0$ (see \eqref{Toch}), where $\varepsilon_n\to 0$ as $ n\to \infty$. Then the inverse problem being solved is conditionally well-posed on $D$.

\label{p3}
Let problem (1) be ill-posed (unstable with respect to data perturbations in $Y$), and for some element $\hat{y}$ from the dataset there is a sequence $\{ {y_n}\}$ such that ${y_n} \in D\backslash {D_Y},\,{y_n} \to \hat y $, and for it the inequality holds
\begin{equation}\label{eq3}
\left\| {{F^{ - 1}}({y_n}) - {F^{ - 1}}(\hat y)} \right\| \ge E = const >\max \left\{\varepsilon_0,\varepsilon _{val}\right\}=\bar{\varepsilon}.
\end{equation}
Then the accuracy of solving the problem using a neural network cannot be better than $E-\bar{\varepsilon}$.
